\par\par \b \uc2 Economy Extends Strong Hiring Growth --- Unemployment stays at 3.6%; labor force declines in tight market for workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Josh Mitchell \par \uc2 1,129 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The U.S. labor market extended a run of rapid job growth last month despite increased economic uncertainty and jittery markets, as employers rushed to serve waves of consumers who are shopping, dining out and traveling more.\par \par \uc2 The economy added 428,000 jobs in April, duplicating March's increase and marking the 12th straight month of gains above 400,000, the Labor Department said Friday. The unemployment rate remained at 3.6%, just a shade above the prepandemic level of 3.5%, a half-century low.\par \par \uc2 Restaurants, hotels and other leisure and hospitality industries led the way in hiring last month, though factories, warehouses and white-collar companies all posted strong job gains. Wage growth cooled a bit but remained robust, with workers' average hourly earnings up 5.5% over the past year, though that isn't enough to keep up with inflation.\par \par \uc2 Friday's report included a puzzling decline in the labor force -- the first since September -- as 363,000 people retreated to the sidelines, shrinking what was already a tight labor pool.\par \par \uc2 Investors, bracing for further interest-rate increases as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  tries to cool the economy and bring down inflation, on Friday extended one of Wall Street's worst selloffs since the pandemic began. The broad S&P 500 fell by 0.6%, capping a fifth consecutive week of losses, the longest streak since June 2011.\par \par \uc2 "No doubt, amid all of the sea of negative news, the labor market continues to be the bright and shining star -- for now," said \uc2 Kathy Bostjancic\uc2 , chief U.S. economist at Oxford Economics. "Domestic demand in the U.S. still remains resilient. We're short of workers and we're short of inventories. That's going to help propel demand for workers."\par \par \uc2 The combination of aggressive hiring and low unemployment will likely embolden the Fed as it shifts its focus from pushing down unemployment to tamping down high inflation. The Fed this week raised its benchmark interest rate by the most since 2000 and plans additional increases in 2022 to rein in the highest inflation in 40 years while aiming to keep job growth steady.\par \par \uc2 Former \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Secretary \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , in a Wall Street Journal interview Friday, raised doubts about the Fed's ability to cool the economy without causing a downturn. "It's definitely odds-off that we will have a soft landing," he said. Mr. Summers added that the risks for the economy are quite substantial and that historically, the current mix of high inflation and low unemployment has led to a recession within two years.\par \par \uc2 While Covid-19 lockdowns in China and Russia's invasion of Ukraine have stoked fears of a global economic slowdown, American consumers are spending as if in boom times. After two years of lockdowns and pandemic-related restrictions, they are going to concerts, dining out and traveling at levels last seen since before the pandemic -- and employers are stepping up hiring as a result.\par \par \uc2 In the year through April, employers added 6.63 million workers to their payrolls -- the biggest 12-month gain, aside from earlier in the pandemic, on records dating to 1939. Employers posted a record-high 11.5 million job openings in March, separate federal figures showed.\par \par \uc2 Yet employment remained down 1.2 million jobs in April compared with prepandemic levels. The biggest obstacle preventing a full recovery and even stronger job growth is a lack of supply as millions of adults remain on the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 Mona Ghattas said she needs an additional half a dozen workers to wait tables at the restaurant behind her pharmacy, Duran Central Pharmacy, and manage the nearby Remedy Coffee shop, all of which she owns and operates in Albuquerque, N.M. She said it is hard to find workers, and when she does, many quit within days or even hours.\par \par \uc2 "It seems like I am continually looking to hire," Ms. Ghattas said. "Turnover is high -- higher than it's ever been. I can hire somebody and they might show up or they might not show up."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ghattas said she has doubled the wages of her waitstaff since 2020 and has added benefits such as boosting healthcare contributions. She needs workers because business at the establishments has been brisk.\par \par \uc2 Customers sometimes face long waits. Ms. Ghattas hands them pagers and beeps them, sometimes an hour later, when their table or order is ready. Due to the labor shortage, she said she has scaled back the restaurant's hours.\par \par \uc2 Millions had joined the labor force over the past year, as workers got vaccinated and employers lured them with higher wages. That progress stalled last month as several hundred thousands workers fell out of the labor force, including many women between ages 25 and 54, according to the Labor Department.\par \par \uc2 Economists were at a loss to explain the labor-force drop, given that Covid-19 infections and deaths have fallen sharply this year, the allure of higher wages and the urgency for a paycheck caused by rapidly rising consumer prices.\par \par \uc2 The labor-force participation rate -- the share of the population 16 and older employed or looking for work -- fell 0.2 percentage point last month to 62.2%, down 1.2 percentage points from its prepandemic level. For prime-age workers -- those aged 25 to 54 -- it only edged down to 82.4% from 82.5%, close to its prepandemic level.\par \par \uc2 There are several reasons why the labor force is so tight. Chief among them: many older workers retired early during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 New research also suggests that while infections and deaths from Covid-19 have fallen sharply this year, many would-be workers are still scared of catching the virus or passing it on to loved ones and are avoiding human contact, including searching for jobs. About 3 million adults are avoiding returning to work because of virus fears, according to the research.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, last month's drop in the labor force could just be a one-month blip rather than the start of a trend.\par \par \uc2 One potential explanation is that many workers have become much more selective about the types of jobs and work environments they seek. Some are no longer willing to work long hours for meager pay doing strenuous tasks. Employers say some workers accept job offers and then never show up.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ghattas's experience helps explain why U.S. gross domestic product fell at a 1.4% annual rate in the first quarter. Companies are eager to boost output but are struggling to find enough workers to serve them -- which ultimately restrains output.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Sarah Chaney Cambon and Harriet Torry contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich515\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7725
