\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- What New Grads Want --- The class of 2022 holds a lot of leverage in a hot hiring market. Money, flexibility and specific assignments are important -- but so is a chance to come into an office.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lindsay Ellis \par \uc2 1,581 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Meet the class of 2022. They are the most in-demand college graduates to enter the job market in years. They have the expectations to match.\par \par \uc2 Grads are seeking more money and flexibility and specifics about likely assignments than previous classes. Some want their employers to take stands on social issues. And after wrapping the last two years of their education under pandemic conditions, they are highly adaptable yet hungry for face-to-face work, training and mentorship.\par \par \uc2 Companies that are struggling to fill open positions have good reason to respond. Many big employers -- from technology giant \uc2 Dell Technologies Inc\uc2 . to professional-services firm \uc2 KPMG\uc2  -- are either stepping up college recruiting or lowering entry-level job requirements to compete.\par \par \uc2 They are also seeking to make up for a surge in turnover over the past year, especially among younger workers, as they seek to bring more staff back to offices and rethink how their workplaces will operate. In a survey of 196 employers during February and March, 16% told the National \uc2 Association of Colleges and Employers\uc2  they would double new-graduate hires from last year.\par \par \uc2 Because new graduates can afford to be choosy, some are taking their time to lock in a new job, recruiters say. About half of new grads who hadn't decided on a job had gotten at least one offer without accepting, according to a March survey of more than 2,500 soon-to-be graduates by Chicago recruiting firm LaSalle Network.\par \par \uc2 Here is a closer look at what these grads want -- and what they are getting:\par \par \uc2 Salary\par \par \uc2 Pay is thus far more generous than some grads were expecting. Liam Burke, 22, graduated early in March from \uc2 Western Washington University\uc2  and got three offers before accepting one. The position he took as a buyer and planner in the aerospace sector, he said, brought a salary higher than the first two offers -- and came in above the range he shared during the interview process.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Burke said it was "nerve-racking" to wait for a best-fit job and let the other offers pass, but "you just have to have this quiet confidence that you're going to do fine."\par \par \uc2 Among those who have accepted jobs, 53% said their starting salary was greater than they expected, while 41% said it was what they anticipated, according to a survey of more than 1,000 college seniors from TimelyMD, a telehealth company providing medical treatment and counseling to students, published in April.\par \par \uc2 Salary is a particularly high priority for new graduates. Raised in the aftermath of the 2008-09 financial crisis, they are part of a generation that experts say is risk-averse and craves financial security. The pandemic, and inflation, sharpened their focus on pay, students and campus career advisers say.\par \par \uc2 Even Wall Street is adjusting. Investment-banking salaries are up 23% for interns, said Terina Matthews-Davis, client director for early careers for \uc2 AMS\uc2 , a company that helps firms recruit new employees. The rise compared with a year ago is even higher for new hires, to $110,000 from $85,000, according to Ms. Matthews-Davis.\par \par \uc2 Employers recruiting in other fields ranging from social sciences to engineering said they planned to raise pay for class of 2022 candidates above rates commanded by the previous class, according to a NACE survey of 124 companies conducted last fall.\par \par \uc2 These boosts are crucial "to get students to accept and not renege on your offer," Ms. Matthews-Davis.\par \par \uc2 Culture and responsibilities\par \par \uc2 It is more than just salary, however. Work responsibilities are important to new grads such as Austin Bowles, 21. The senior at \uc2 Coastal Carolina University\uc2  said he saw "tons and tons" of listings for his desired first job as an electrician for theater companies, and weeded out postings that were unclear about what the job actually entailed.\par \par \uc2 He wanted to avoid the limitations of a prior gig where he was required to help attendees park in a large gravel lot before the curtain rose. He recently accepted a job at Northern Stage, a theater in White River Junction, Vt., as an assistant master electrician.\par \par \uc2 Grads want the same specifics from bigger employers. Candidates for positions at \uc2 Dell\uc2  want to know who they will be working for and what projects they will tackle, according to Jennifer Newbill, the company's director of emerging talent. \uc2 Dell\uc2  is planning to hire 1,580 new graduates in 2022 for full-time work, a 19% increase from last year.\par \par \uc2 KPMG\uc2  gets questions about the company's work on social issues, according to the managing partner who oversees the company's hiring of college grads. That executive, James Powell, said some prospective hires want to know about an organization for LGBTQ employees that Mr. Powell lists on his \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  profile.\par \par \uc2 KPMG\uc2  is planning to hire 4,500 college recruits to full-time positions, one of its largest new-hire classes ever and about a 20% increase from last year. "They know they have options," said Mr. Powell, the firm's national partner in charge of university talent acquisition. "The competition is really fierce."\par \par \uc2 One management-consulting company, \uc2 Oliver Wyman\uc2 , tried to recruit new candidates in March with a San Francisco development and networking event called the Diversity Leadership Summit. The audience was composed of sophomores and juniors from diverse backgrounds, and company representatives discussed consulting and careers as well as sustainability. They finished one session by announcing that the firm would plant 64 trees, one for each attendee, in students' names.\par \par \uc2 One senior who said she pays attention to a company's demographics is Niha Sheikh, a 22-year-old at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2 . She said she has connected with current and former employees on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  to get an unfiltered view of a workplace's culture.\par \par \uc2 "I don't see myself being super comfortable working in an environment where I am the only person of color," she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Sheikh, a practicing Muslim, said she closes many job interviews by asking if she can have space and time in an office to conduct her daily prayers. She is looking for a role in human resources or in a school, either in the classroom or in administration.\par \par \uc2 "I think the responses to questions like that are very telling to me about the company's morals and values," she said.\par \par \uc2 Flexibility\par \par \uc2 Many new graduates are hungry for in-person interactions with their future colleagues, having spent years in virtual internships and courses.\par \par \uc2 Eugene Thomas, a 22-year-old \uc2 Yale University\uc2  senior from New York City, said working on-site was a priority. He accepted a product engineering job with farm-equipment giant \uc2 Deere & Co\uc2 . after a remote internship last summer and will participate in a two-year rotation at the company.\par \par \uc2 "I don't even think I entertained the option of remote work," he said. Remote school, he said, "felt like I was swimming in molasses or something. . . . I felt a lack of motivation."\par \par \uc2 Flexibility is still a key component for Mr. Thomas, who said he was able to get deadlines pushed back during his internship last summer after his mother was hospitalized. Other grads said the same; 68% of seniors surveyed by TimelyMD cited the importance of flexible work hours.\par \par \uc2 Some want that flexibility to include a choice between on-site and remote work. In fact, 60% of 571 respondents to a March and April survey of seniors from recruiting-software firm \uc2 iCIMS\uc2  said they wanted a hybrid arrangement.\par \par \uc2 Yet new grads aren't as excited about full-time remote arrangements. Just 11% are interested in that setup, according to LaSalle Network's March survey of more than 2,500 soon-to-be graduates.\par \par \uc2 "I feel like I will get bored of just being at home all the time," said Sydney Washington, a senior at the \uc2 University of North Texas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Networking\par \par \uc2 Students say they are looking for jobs on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  and on organizations' job sites, in addition to connections from professors. They turn to social media and campus career centers for guidance.\par \par \uc2 "They don't really need our help in leveraging the digital tools in front of them," said Julia Overton-Healy, director of career services at St. John Fisher College in Rochester, N.Y. "They need more help in how to engage in relationship building -- how to have those first-contact conversations with an alum, for example."\par \par \uc2 Sanja Marinovic, 21, initially struck out last fall when she applied to jobs in food safety and quality assurance. But she read career advice on Reddit, where users suggested seeking informational interviews first. Those introductory conversations helped her know what certifications to cite.\par \par \uc2 For the job she eventually accepted -- a food-safety auditing post in Los Angeles -- she did a phone screen on a Friday and then did a panel interview the following Tuesday, getting the formal offer the next day.\par \par \uc2 She was surprised by the speed, given how long other hiring teams had taken with her application. Her career adviser, at the SUNY College of Environmental Science and Forestry in Syracuse, N.Y., reviewed the offer with her and said the fast pace was normal in a tight labor market.\par \par \uc2 "Once there was a company that was interested in me, they really seemed interested in me," she said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich429\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6435
