\par\par \b \uc2 C-Suite Strategies (A Special Report) --- The Problem With Working on Weekends: A new study says that even when you choose to do it, you resent it\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anne Kadet \par \uc2 692 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Flexibility has become the mantra of the hybrid workplace: Employees will be happier if they are allowed to set their own hours. But a new study suggests that may not necessarily be true.\par \par \uc2 The study found that people working weekends and holidays enjoy their work less and experience decreased motivation -- even when they choose the schedule themselves.\par \par \uc2 Prompted first by the pandemic, and then by the current worker shortage, more companies are offering employees the ability to set their own hours -- a perk that was expected to improve the work experience because it gives people more autonomy and control. "And yet we see again and again and again that people don't experience the benefits that we're supposed to be experiencing," says Laura Giurge, an assistant professor of behavioral science at the \uc2 London School of Economics and Political Science\uc2 , and a co-author of the study.\par \par \uc2 Overall, researchers found that working outside of the traditional 9-to-5 puts a damper on intrinsic motivation -- a measure of how much people work simply because they enjoy the task at hand and find it meaningful -- for a simple reason: Even if you're choosing to work on a Saturday, you can't help ruminating about how everyone else is having more fun at the pool, or relaxing on the couch.\par \par \uc2 In one study, the researchers measured participants' intrinsic motivation on an ordinary Monday by asking them questions about why they were working. Did they find their work interesting? Enjoyable? Meaningful? The researchers conducted the same assessment the next Monday, which was a holiday. People working on the holiday reported a 9% drop in intrinsic motivation, even though they scheduled work for that day.\par \par \uc2 The same effect was found in a study comparing those who chose to work on a Tuesday and then on the following Saturday. Working on a Saturday produced an 11% drop in intrinsic motivation and enjoyment.\par \par \uc2 And this demotivating effect isn't confined to the day at hand. Working nonstandard hours can have a long-term corrosive effect on work enjoyment, says study co-author Kaitlin Woolley, an associate professor of marketing at the SC Johnson Cornell College of Business. Dr. Woolley, who, along with Dr. Giurge spent a lot of weekend and holiday time working on the study, says that in supplemental analysis, the authors found that shift employees and others who regularly work when everyone else has time off are chronically less motivated and enjoy their jobs less.\par \par \uc2 So can workers do anything about this? While it may sound simplistic, it is actually helpful to pretend it isn't happening. The studies show that those reminded of the fact that they are working on a holiday or weekend suffer more.\par \par \uc2 It also helps to connect with others who are sharing the misery. Staying in contact with co-workers who are also tied to their laptops over the holiday mitigates the sense of being isolated and uniquely burdened.\par \par \uc2 Another tactic: Deliberately consider the benefits of working nonstandard hours. The researchers found that workers who were prompted to consider the upside of working on the weekend -- such as catching up and completing tasks they've been putting off -- enjoyed a 13% increase in their work enjoyment and intrinsic motivation.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, though, the message of the study is that flexibility comes with trade-offs, and not just for individuals but for society, which benefits from the idea of a collective time off. After all, it isn't much fun to have Tuesday free when there's no one around for coffee and a movie.\par \par \uc2 "While it can feel illicit, or sort of exciting, it can also prompt you to feel a little bit of guilt -- that you have off, and other people are working," says Dr. Woolley. "If you have off and everyone else is working, you can't really get the benefits."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ms. Kadet is a writer in Brooklyn, N.Y. She can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220505ei5500004\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
