\par\par \b \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  Allows Staff To Choose Worksite\b0\par\par\uc2 By Preetika Rana \par \uc2 457 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 April 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Airbnb Inc\uc2 . is telling employees they can now work from almost anywhere in the world without a pay cut in a move that could entice other companies to follow suit and bolster the company's business.\par \par \uc2 Chief Executive \uc2 Brian Chesky\uc2  unveiled the new work-from-home policy in a note to staff Thursday. The policy is designed to retain top talent and encourage other companies to offer greater flexibility, he said.\par \par \uc2 As the health crisis has reshaped the way people think about work and travel, companies are feeling their way to new, postpandemic norms. Some are deciding that having teams working together again in offices is the best way forward. \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  is backing a more open view of where people can work, adding fuel to a trend that could benefit its business if it leads to more people working from its rental properties.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  parent \uc2 Alphabet Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 . are urging workers to return to the office a few days a week. \uc2 Twitter Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  parent \uc2 Meta Platforms Inc\uc2 . are offering greater flexibility. But myriad tax and other rules have generally meant that employees have had to stick to working in countries where they have legal residency. \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  is trying to go further than most to help its employees choose their own new normal.\par \par \uc2 Under \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 's new policy, pay scales for all U.S. and Canadian employees will be standardized at the top-paying tier instead of using location-based tiers. The home-rental company also said that its more than 6,000 employees can work for as long as three months in a year from 170 of the countries and regions in which the company operates.\par \par \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  is creating a 20-person team that works with employees to navigate the payroll and tax complications. "It's basically a giant paperwork nuisance and no one likes to put up with it," Mr. Chesky said. The company is separately lobbying governments around the world to try to make it easier for travelers to work remotely for extended periods.\par \par \uc2 How companies compensate relocating workers can be tricky. Tech companies including \uc2 Facebook\uc2  said early on in the pandemic that they would adjust pay for people moving to less expensive cities. Others like \uc2 Reddit Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Zillow Group\uc2  decided to do away with location-based pay. Cloud-software company \uc2 Okta Inc\uc2 . initially cut pay for relocating workers but later reversed its decision.\par \par \uc2 The ability to work remotely and travel to places beyond crowded city centers made \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  a pandemic winner while hotel chains in big cities suffered. \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 's 2021 revenue topped 2019. Chains like \uc2 Marriott International Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Hilton Worldwide Holdings Inc\uc2 . haven't bounced back to prepandemic revenue.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220429ei4t0001y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
