\par\par \b \uc2 Jorts the Cat vs. Scabby the Rat: Labor Needs New Mascots --- Renewed interest in union activism means work for a fluffy feline\b0\par\par\uc2 By Allison Prang \par \uc2 1,007 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 April 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Preparing to support some workers going on strike last month, Courtney John, a parks and recreation worker in the Minneapolis area, designed her picket line placard. On it was an image of a chunky, ginger-haired fellow who has become one of organized labor's biggest cheerleaders.\par \par \uc2 He isn't a member of any union himself. Technically, he can't be. He's a cat.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. is seeing a renewed interest in labor activism at corporations small and large, including recent union votes at \uc2 Starbucks Corp\uc2 ., and \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . More activity also means more goofy mascots.\par \par \uc2 Jorts, the fluffy, pro-labor orange tabby, has graced picket signs after recently achieving internet fame.\par \par \uc2 "I would definitely consider him an icon of the labor movement," says Ms. John, 34 years old.\par \par \uc2 An employee brought a live chicken in a cage, with a sign reading "We Work For Chicken Feed" to a labor rally last week in Minnesota, says Matt Lelou, president of a union there. "People took turns rolling him around the rally," he says of the fowl. "He was hanging out in his cage, pecking and doing whateverchickens do."\par \par \uc2 Last July, the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  ruled 3-1 that a union had the right to display a "12-foot inflatable rat with red eyes, fangs, and claws ('Scabby the Rat')" targeting an employer near the public entrance to a trade show. An RV-components company had filed the complaint.\par \par \uc2 The dissenting \uc2 NLRB\uc2  member wrote that "the rat in particular, a dominating physical presence, plainly created a symbolic confrontation."\par \par \uc2 An enormous inflatable rat popped up on the campus of Truman State University in Kirksville, Mo., in December when a group of maintenance and custodial workers protested.\par \par \uc2 "It was a little odd," says Travis Miles, a university spokesman. "I was surprised by how tall it was."\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, he says, "On a college campus, there's always weird things going on."\par \par \uc2 Union organizers in Chicago helped popularize "Scabby the Rat" decades ago, and the scowling, gray blowup rodents draw attention to companies being picketed. An Illinois company, Big Sky Balloons & Searchlights, says in its "Rats Brochure" that the rodents are available from "6<prime> to 25<prime> tall!" and suggests you "Order your rat today!" (The company also touts "Our new Corporate Fat Cat" and "Our new Corporate Greedy Pig" inflatables.)\par \par \uc2 More than 200 of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's 9,000 U.S. corporate stores have petitioned for union elections since last year. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees at another warehouse in New York are set to vote this week on whether to unionize. Jorts the cat has weighed in on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  at unionization pushes at both companies. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  didn't return requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Restaurant workers, teachers, university instructors, grocery workers and others have also staged walkouts and strikes.\par \par \uc2 In the past year or so, "groups just starting to get formed -- grad students, musicians --they don't have a rat. They'll call and say 'Hey, do you guys have a rat?' " says Ed Maher, spokesman for the \uc2 International Union of Operating Engineers\uc2 , Local 150 in Illinois. "Our answer is always, 'Yeah, we have a rat.'"\par \par \uc2 To meet new mascot needs, the \uc2 Chicago Federation of Labor\uc2  got a large inflatable loaner Scabby two months ago and will "be adding another rat to the fleet," says President \uc2 Bob Reiter\uc2 . The federation has about 300 affiliated unions.\par \par \uc2 "He was double booked this week, but we were able to navigate it," Mr. Reiter says.\par \par \uc2 Hollywood assistants have used \uc2 Instagram\uc2  stories to organize a labor walkout. And last October, some employees at the \uc2 Hooters\uc2  restaurant chain went on TikTok calling new uniform shorts too short.\par \par \uc2 The brief publicity got their boss's attention. \uc2 Hooters\uc2  soon put out a statement, clarifying that the shorts were optional.\par \par \uc2 "Our Hooters Girls are providing input at every stage," said the October statement.\par \par \uc2 The world-wide web first heard about Jorts in December, when a Reddit user posted about cats at a place where the person worked, felines named Jorts and Jean. Jorts, the poster said, was the dim one, struggling to open doors. The post garnered huge interest on social media and was retweeted thousands of times, with Jorts becoming something of a relatable underdog (or cat).\par \par \uc2 That same month, a new \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account appeared -- @JortsTheCat -- and, it turned out, Jorts was a union cat, frequently posting in support of workers actions. "2022 has already blessed the labor movement with a new icon," a blog devoted to unions said in February. "Yes, it's time to talk about Jorts."\par \par \uc2 Jorts now has more than 178,000 \uc2 Twitter\uc2  followers -- more than labor union giant the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Let's rest up for more winning," the cat tweeted in early April, around the time it was announced that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers at a warehouse on Staten Island voted in favor of unionizing. The cat urged the public to attend a labor rally at a Pittsburgh Starbucks, inspired Jorts signs, and trolled California's Democratic governor.\par \par \uc2 While there is presumably a human behind the online presence (what cat would stay awake long enough to send all those tweets?), \uc2 Sara Nelson\uc2 , the international president of the \uc2 Association of Flight Attendants-CWA\uc2 , \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , believes it's key that people think Jorts is a feline. "Jorts just has to remain Jorts the cat," Ms. Nelson says. "The second that we start trying to assign a human to Jorts all the magic will fall apart."\par \par \uc2 In an interview via direct messages through his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account, Jorts declined to reveal his real identity and stayed in character as a cat.\par \par \uc2 "We are worker cats, so we stand in solidarity, even though nobody has figured out how to really exploit the labor of cats," Jorts said. He claims to send out his own tweets with his toe beans, or the small pads of skin on the bottom of cats' paws.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220427ei4r00028\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
