\par\par \b \uc2 Cloud Services Propel \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  --- Businesses continue to benefit from the shift to remote work during the pandemic\b0\par\par\uc2 By Aaron Tilley \par \uc2 511 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 April 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Microsoft Corp\uc2 .'s revenue and profit rose last quarter as demand for its cloud services and software continued to climb with the shift to remote work triggered by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 For the quarter through March, the Redmond, Wash., company said revenue rose 18% from a year earlier to $49.4 billion as net income rose 8% to $16.7 billion. Analysts polled by \uc2 FactSet\uc2  had predicted it would announce revenue of around $49 billion and net income of around $16 billion.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  shares fell $10.50, or 3.7%, to $270.22 on Tuesday. In after-hours trading, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  shares rose 4..6% after the company unveiled a strong outlook on sales for the current quarter.\par \par \uc2 Through the pandemic, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and other business-software companies experienced booming stock prices and sales as organizations around the world used more digital tools to help with remote working. This has bolstered demand for \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's office applications as well as cloud-infrastructure services.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  remains the second-largest cloud-infrastructure vendor behind \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 ., but the company has been gaining market share by using its leadership in office applications as leverage to grab big deals for its Azure cloud. It had nearly 20% of the market in 2020, according to research firm \uc2 Gartner Inc\uc2 . -- well behind \uc2 Amazon Web Services\uc2 ' 40% but up from 7% in 2016.\par \par \uc2 The company's overall cloud business rose 32% with $23.4 billion in sales as its rival to \uc2 Amazon\uc2  cloud infrastructure service grew by 46%.\par \par \uc2 Analysts and investors have been looking for signs that demand is cooling. Software shares have been sliding this year, with \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  down around 20%, in line with the Nasdaq Composite Index.\par \par \uc2 Results were largely in line with expectations. There were concerns that a slowdown may be on the horizon after the previous quarterly earnings were announced in January because the growth of \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's cloud service Azure for that quarter hadn't been as strong as expected. Growth seems to be back on track now, analysts said, showing companies continue to spend on cloud services.\par \par \uc2 "This is a good bellwether for the rest of software," said \uc2 Rishi Jaluria\uc2 , an analyst with \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 . "There's no big slowdown in the spending environment."\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  is showing little sign of slowdown despite the war in Ukraine, inflation and other factors which have been hurting growth in many industries. The company expects its sales for the current quarter to be between $52.4 billion and $53.2 billion. Analysts had been expecting sales of around $52.8 billion, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . Chief Financial Officer \uc2 Amy Hood\uc2  said the war in Ukraine is expected to have around a $110 million sales impact on the company in the current quarter.\par \par \uc2 The videogame arm generated close to 10% of revenue in 2021. The company is trying to build it into another core business, by using its cloud infrastructure to take the lead in an emerging cloud-gaming sector.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich471\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7065
