\par\par \b \uc2 The Future of Everything: Cities & Real Estate --- City Planning: How Towns Will Fill the Coffers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Grant \par \uc2 856 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Many U.S. cities were enjoying strong growth in the years leading up to the pandemic, with rents, hotel bookings, sale prices and tax collections rising as urban centers became popular with young workers and the businesses that wanted to hire them.\par \par \uc2 Those good times ended in March 2020, when much of the country went into lockdown mode. By the end of that year, many cities were forced to furlough large portions of their workforces and cut programs as tax revenues from sources such as retail sales, hotels and property shrank. Now, city leaders are looking for new and innovative ways to bolster their budgets and restore financial health longer term.\par \par \uc2 The federal government's $1.9 trillion Covid-19 relief plan passed last year helped cities close big holes in their budgets for the short term. But the future is clouded as no one knows how remote and hybrid work models adopted by thousands of companies will affect office vacancy rates, business travel and business-district retailers.\par \par \uc2 Here are some of the key strategies city leaders are considering to shore up their prospects for the future.\par \par \uc2 Shifting tax burdens to homeowners: City officials throughout the country are looking at raising residential property taxes to make up for declining commercial property values. For some fortunate cities, this shift is being eased, for now, by hot housing markets that are driving up values.\par \par \uc2 In Philadelphia, the city's coffers were hammered during the pandemic because the city gets 40% of its budget from a wage tax that workers who live outside the city don't have to pay if they're told to work remotely. Philadelphia Mayor \uc2 Jim Kenney\uc2  and the city council last year created a working group to study ways to revise the city's tax structure, possibly by relying less on wage taxes and more on property taxes.\par \par \uc2 The revitalization of many cities in the last decade was largely funded by the commercial property taxes, notes Ronald Rakow, formerly Boston's assessing department commissioner and now a fellow at the Lincoln Institute of Land Policy, a think tank. "Cities are going to face the choice of either having to cut services or to raise more revenue from residents."\par \par \uc2 Boosting entertainment, sports and gaming: Before the pandemic, cities used sports, entertainment districts and casinos as a way to attract nonresidents and their pocketbooks. Now cities are looking to expand existing attractions and create new ones partly to make up for lost visitors and tax dollars from business travel, conventions and other sources.\par \par \uc2 In Cincinnati, the city closed a street during the pandemic and allowed people to walk around with alcoholic drinks in a sports and entertainment district named the Banks. The 85-acre district, on the banks of the Ohio River, has lost much of the large lunch crowd it used to get from office workers in downtown Cincinnati. Weekend and evening visitors who buy specially marked cups to use in the district have more than made up for that loss, says Tracy Schwegmann of Jeffrey R. Anderson Real Estate Inc., the district's retail manager. "We've gone through one million cups."\par \par \uc2 A number of states and cities are taking a new look at casino development, well aware that commercial gambling generated $11.6 billion in state and local taxes nationally in 2021, according to the \uc2 American Gaming Association\uc2 . Voters in four Virginia cities approved casinos in November 2020. Chicago is talking to developers about the city's plans to open its first casino as early as 2023.\par \par \uc2 Rethinking neighborhoods: Cities have long focused their economic development efforts on attracting and retaining big employers. But as companies increasingly shift to remote and hybrid work models, cities are investing instead in attracting and retaining residents.\par \par \uc2 Many are doing this partly by allowing restaurants that expanded to curbsides during the pandemic to keep that new real estate That's helping small businesses, while making neighborhoods more vibrant, city officials say.\par \par \uc2 Converting obsolete office buildings into apartments is another strategy. New York Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 's housing plan announced earlier this month includes $100 million for conversions of hotel and office buildings to residential properties.\par \par \uc2 Using the advantage: Not all cities are getting hurt. Miami, for instance, has a $140 million surplus in its bank account and projected surpluses of $20 million annually thanks partly to businesses relocating to Florida during the pandemic from high-tax states like New York and California.\par \par \uc2 Miami Mayor \uc2 Francis Suarez\uc2  says those surpluses may enable the city to move ahead with plans for such capital projects as a proposed six-mile high-speed tunnel for \uc2 Tesla\uc2  electric vehicles with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's Boring Co. The company earlier this year submitted a proposal for the project that it said would get built much faster than one using traditional tunneling methods, Mr. Suarez says. Boring Co. didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 "We have the capacity to do major capital projects without raising taxes that other cities just can't do," Mr. Suarez said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220414ei4e0000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
