\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Attack Hardens Concerns About Public Safety\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Grant \par \uc2 595 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The attack on a subway during rush hour is adding to the perception that New York and other cities are unsafe, employers and others say, a key reason they are having trouble persuading people to return to the office.\par \par \uc2 Even before Tuesday's shooting on a Brooklyn subway train, employees who have worked remotely throughout the Covid-19 pandemic were listing public safety as one of the top reasons they were reluctant to return to offices. Now, the images of carnage erupting on a train and platform during a morning commute are giving workers another reason to stay at home, particularly those who take public transportation.\par \par \uc2 Such health and safety concerns are hard to overcome from an employer's perspective "because you're telling people they have to put their own personal health and safety at risk to come back to a workplace where they're likely to do the same thing they would do at home," said \uc2 Brian Kropp\uc2 , chief of human-resources research for advisory firm \uc2 Gartner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Quality-of-life issues like crime and the higher visibility of homeless people are playing a major role throughout the U.S. in slowing the return to offices to a pace that is well below what business leaders and city officials say they would like to see. The fear of infection from Covid-19 doesn't appear to be as much of a concern, as evidenced by the much-stronger return rate to crowded events like basketball games and airports, according to \uc2 Kastle Systems\uc2 , a firm that has been tracking the return-to-office rate.\par \par \uc2 In New York, for example, workers listed safety, homelessness and mentally ill people -- particularly on the subway -- as the top reasons they were resisting returning to the office, according to a survey of more than 9,300 people by the Partnership for New York City, a business group.\par \par \uc2 Employers are sensitive to worker concerns partly because the labor market is so tight, according to consultants who work with corporate human-resources departments. The perception of crime in the city, now magnified by the subway attack, "gives employees the excuse to say: 'You can't bring me back to the office. It's not safe,'" said \uc2 Kathryn Wylde\uc2 , the Partnership's chief executive.\par \par \uc2 An average of 43% of the workforce returned to the office in the first week of April in the 10 major cities monitored by Kastle, which records building-access-card swipes nationwide. That is the highest rate so far during the pandemic, but less than 3 percentage points higher than it was in early December before the Omicron variant hit.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the pandemic, the return rate has been lower in cities like New York and San Francisco that depend on public transportation. Even as concern about contracting Covid-19 on public transportation has declined, worries about crime and homelessness on subways have kept people away, said \uc2 Mark Ein\uc2 , Kastle's chairman.\par \par \uc2 Homelessness and crime, of course, were issues before the Covid-19 pandemic. But before the pandemic, working from home wasn't an option for most employees. "There wasn't pushback before because there was no Plan B," Mr. Kropp said.\par \par \uc2 The low return-to-office rate is having harmful effects on city budgets and small businesses that depend on commuters. The federal government's $1.9 trillion Covid-19 relief plan passed last year helped cities plug existing holes in their budgets, but not future holes that might occur from lower property- and sales-tax collections.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220414ei4e00019\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
