\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Deal Ends Strike by Teachers in Sacramento\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sara Randazzo \par \uc2 341 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 April 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Thousands of students returned to classrooms Monday in Sacramento, Calif., after an eight-day teacher and staff strike ended in a tentative deal over the weekend to boost pay and improve health benefits.\par \par \uc2 Unions representing 4,600 teachers and staff in Sacramento City Unified School District walked out in March over working conditions that they said left hundreds of vacancies unfilled, resulting in students routinely being without a regular teacher or substitute.\par \par \uc2 The deal, which the unions and school board will vote on later this week, includes annual 4% pay raises for teachers and staff, plus back pay retroactive to 2019-20 and bonuses. Substitute teachers will get 25% higher pay, and the staff contract includes several provisions aimed at recruiting more bus drivers, including a $5,000 signing bonus.\par \par \uc2 The district conceded on one major sticking point: to continue to fund its current two types of healthcare plans for teachers.\par \par \uc2 "It can't magically overnight fill every vacancy, but it'll stop the bleeding," said David Fisher, president of the Sacramento City Teachers Association, of the agreement.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of teachers, staff and families throughout the district rallied Friday in front of the district offices, yelling demands to Superintendent Jorge Aguilar through a bullhorn and encouraging drivers in passing cars to honk in support. A group of parents camped out, sleepingovernight inside the district office for several days to increase pressure on the negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Hours of talks over the weekend broke the impasse and led to the tentative deal reached late Sunday night, Mr. Fisher said. If approved, the contract would be in effect through June 2023.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Aguilar said in a statement that the district, which has around 40,000 students, "did all that we could to demonstrate how highly we value our employees."\par \par \uc2 Labor strife has hit districts elsewhere in the U.S. In Minneapolis, classrooms were closed for most of March before a deal over pay and benefits brought teachers back to work last week.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220405ei450000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
