\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Amazon Union Vote Set to Begin\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sebastian Herrera \par \uc2 941 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 March 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . over the next month will face union elections at separate warehouses in New York City, a union-friendly area that has challenged the e-commerce giant in the past.\par \par \uc2 Current and former workers at the company's largest Staten Island warehouse are leading the effort to become the first group of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees to unionize in the U.S. They are operating without the backing of a major labor union, an uncommon tactic, but one that organizers believe will win support from workers.\par \par \uc2 Labor experts say the grass-roots effort could pose challenges for union supporters, as potential costs could escalate for appeals or in procedural questions. Some employees say they support the approach and how it has been handled locally, although others say they have concerns about the experience of those managing the effort.\par \par \uc2 The vote at a warehouse named JFK8, which employs about 7,500 people, will take place March 25 to 30. Workers at a different Staten Island facility named LDJ5, which employs about 1,500 workers, will vote the week of April 25.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  pays a starting salary that averages about $18 an hour and has said it already provides much of what organizers are seeking and prefers to negotiate with employees directly. In a statement, the company said its focus "remains on working directly with our team to continue making \uc2 Amazon\uc2  a great place to work."\par \par \uc2 The pro-union campaigners say they hope to improve pay, benefits and working conditions. During the pandemic, employees at the JFK8 warehouse emerged as some of \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's most vocal and organized workers, holding protests calling for safer conditions as the company sought to meet a record number of orders during health lockdowns.\par \par \uc2 Union organizers at the facilities say they hope the Staten Island votes will be the first of many successful organizing campaigns at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , following a trend at \uc2 Starbucks Corp\uc2 . that began when baristas voted to unionize in Buffalo, N.Y., in December. That effort has spread to other locations.\par \par \uc2 "We hope to be like the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  movement and branch out across the nation," said Chris Smalls, a former \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employee heading the union effort in Staten Island. Mr. Smalls was fired by the company in 2020 in what he said was retaliation for his attempts to organize workers. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said he violated safety protocols related to Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 Both elections in Staten Island are part of a broader labor movement that has drawn significant attention as \uc2 Amazon\uc2  became the nation's second-largest private employer and expanded its operations around the country. The U.S. is experiencing a tight labor market that has given employees more negotiating power.\par \par \uc2 A mail-in revote is also under way at a company facility in Bessemer, Ala. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  overwhelmingly prevailed in a contest at that facility last year, with about 71% of workers who cast ballots voting against unionization. But later, the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  found \uc2 Amazon\uc2  exerted unlawful influence over workers.\par \par \uc2 The election a year ago in Alabama became the first campaign of its scale at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . In Staten Island, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is confronting a group of workers who have been vocal and organized for years in one of America's most union-friendly states.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, the company canceled plans for a large expansion known as "HQ2" in New York City after activists, elected officials and unions opposed the deal, which would have given \uc2 Amazon\uc2  nearly $3 billion in government incentives in exchange for creating about 25,000 jobs in the city. Around the same period, the Retail, Wholesale and Department Store Union tried to organize workers in Staten Island, an effort that eventually fizzled.\par \par \uc2 But protests by \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers in the borough endured, and the tension heightened when the Covid-19 pandemic started.\par \par \uc2 Early in the health crisis, employees at JFK8 were among groups of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers throughout the nation to hold facility walkouts to protest working conditions. Many employees said at the time that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  didn't take enough precautions to protect workers while it gave priority to meeting customer demand amid an uptick in orders.\par \par \uc2 Initially, the company struggled with communicating about employees who had tested positive for Covid-19 and equipping workers with sufficient protective gear, according to workers. Performance quotas, including mandates that some employees sort or prepare hundreds of packages in an hour, remained unchanged.\par \par \uc2 During the pandemic, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  raised wages, introduced new safety training and said it would focus on building better relationships with its workers as part of an effort to become "Earth's Best Employer," a goal set by founder \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  last year.\par \par \uc2 After his firing, Mr. Smalls, the union leader, continued to protest outside of the facility, and last April, he formed the "Amazon Labor Union," a group of current and former employees that has run the campaign in Staten Island.\par \par \uc2 The group has campaigned almost daily for the past year, passing out food and water to employees and hosting small rallies. Mr. Smalls, who said he has remained unemployed since being fired, said the group is funded entirely through donations. As of this month, Amazon Labor Union had raised more than $100,000 through \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  pages.\par \par \uc2 In Staten Island and Alabama, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has held meetings to discourage workers from siding with unions. Company representatives in New York often point out the infant status of Amazon Labor Union and bring up potential union fees workers could have to pay, according to interviews with employees. \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's high turnover rates also make it difficult for union organizers to gain and sustain support, labor experts say.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220324ei3o0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
