\par\par \b \uc2 Meta\uc2 's Executives Go Remote For Their Own Work\b0\par\par\uc2 By Salvador Rodriguez and Deepa Seetharaman \par \uc2 885 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 March 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As more companies begin their return to the office, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  parent \uc2 Meta Platforms Inc\uc2 . is becoming more remote than ever before.\par \par \uc2 The company's management team, including Chief Executive \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , is scattering to locations far from its Silicon Valley headquarters in an extreme test of the limits of remote work.\par \par \uc2 Naomi Gleit, the company's head of product and one of its longest-tenured employees, has relocated to New York. Chief Marketing Officer \uc2 Alex Schultz\uc2  plans to move to the U.K., and Guy Rosen, the company's vice president of integrity, will be moving to Israel in the near future, according to a company spokesman.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile \uc2 Javier Olivan\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chief growth officer, has split his time between California and Europe but is planning to spend more time abroad, the spokesman said. \uc2 Meta\uc2  last week said that it will be doubling its Madrid office in Mr. Olivan's home country of Spain to add 2,000 people over the next five years.\par \par \uc2 Adam Mosseri, the head of \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , in recent months has been traveling and working remotely from locations including Hawaii, Los Angeles and Cape Cod, according to people familiar with the matter and the executive's social-media posts. The spokesman said that Mr. Mosseri has no plans to relocate permanently.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zuckerberg has also been spending more time away from the company's headquarters in Menlo Park, Calif., according to people familiar with the matter. Mr. Zuckerberg regularly spends extended periods at his compound in Hawaii and his other homes outside the Bay Area, the people said.\par \par \uc2 "The past few years have brought new possibilities around the ways we connect and work," said \uc2 Meta\uc2  spokesman Tracy Clayton. "We believe that how people work is far more important than where they work from." He said Mr. Zuckerberg plans to spend more than half his time in California and work remotely the rest of the year.\par \par \uc2 In the short term Mr. Zuckerberg will also be without the counsel of his longtime No. 2, Chief Operating Officer \uc2 Sheryl Sandberg\uc2 , who is taking a sabbatical this spring, as part of the company's program to offer 30 days of paid leave every five years.\par \par \uc2 The company's embrace of remote work follows its name change from \uc2 Facebook Inc\uc2 . to \uc2 Meta\uc2  in October, when the company signaled that it thinks virtual first interactions are the future of the internet. For work purposes, in particular, the company says it has invested in a range of technologies to make remote work more efficient, including videoconferencing and enterprise software tools.\par \par \uc2 The executive exodus from Menlo Park comes as \uc2 Meta\uc2  navigates significant challenges to its business.\par \par \uc2 The company's stock price is down more than 32% since it announced its 2021 fourth-quarter earnings on Feb. 2. That plunge has cost the company more than $300 billion in market value. The drop was a result of a number of challenges facing \uc2 Meta\uc2 , including growing competition by Chinese rival TikTok, a declining user base and continuing impact to the company's advertising business from privacy changes to \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 .'s iOS that \uc2 Meta\uc2  said it expects will cost the company some $10 billion this year.\par \par \uc2 Having this many top executives spread across so many different time zones is a concern considering that the company's most recent quarterly results seemed to call for more of an all-hands-on-deck situation, said David Heger, an analyst at Edward Jones.\par \par \uc2 "Considering the juncture that the company's at right at the moment, it may not be the ideal time to be experimenting with your top managers working remotely," he said.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  in June said it would allow all full-time employees to apply to work from home if their jobs allowed it, even as it has continued to add office space to accommodate its growing workforce.\par \par \uc2 This policy doesn't apply to all employees. Those who work on hardware devices or the company's data-center infrastructure are still expected to come to the office.\par \par \uc2 Apart from these workers, \uc2 Meta\uc2  has shifted to a hybrid model that allows most employees, including top executives, to work from wherever they deem best.March 28 is the company's return-to-work date for those who are expected or want to go back to the office.\par \par \uc2 With \uc2 Meta\uc2  pushing to build more products for its users that allow them to interact online virtually in the so-called metaverse, having executives spread out geographically might be an asset, said Stephen Lee, founding principal at Logan Capital, a registered investment adviser that has bought \uc2 Meta\uc2  shares on behalf of clients.\par \par \uc2 "If you're trying to develop the metaverse and develop workplace systems, learning by doing is probably not a bad way to do it," Mr. Lee said.\par \par \uc2 Having executives working together in person allows them to collaborate and build trust with one another, said \uc2 Bill George\uc2 , senior fellow at \uc2 Harvard Business School\uc2  where he is a professor of management practice. Mr. George said he thinks it serves the company better to have senior executives close to their team members.\par \par \uc2 "I've been to Hawaii, and I can tell you, you don't get a lot of work done over there," Mr. George said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220324ei3o00023\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
