\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 Disney\uc2  Staff Protest LGBT Stand\b0\par\par\uc2 By Robbie Whelan and Katherine Sayre \par \uc2 636 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 March 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Small groups of \uc2 Walt Disney Co\uc2 . employees across the U.S. took Tuesday off from work and gathered to protest what they described as the company's continued failure to support LGBT employees.\par \par \uc2 The walkouts mark the beginning of a third week of turmoil inside the company, as its leadership struggles to contain fallout from its response to a Florida education bill that many employees said targeted the LGBT community.\par \par \uc2 The Parental Rights in Education bill, passed March 8 by the Florida Senate, prohibits classroom instruction on gender identity or sexual orientation for children in the third grade or lower and limits such instruction for older students.\par \par \uc2 The issue has emerged as a challenge for \uc2 Disney\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Bob Chapek\uc2  as he enters the third and final year of his contract. Employees, fans, shareholders and elected officials from both sides of the political spectrum have slammed \uc2 Disney\uc2  first for failing to take a public stance on the bill, then for opposing it only after it had passed and while the company was under heavy public pressure to do so.\par \par \uc2 Disney\uc2  sought to head off the protests at a virtual town-hall meeting held Monday, where Mr. Chapek assured employees that the company would use its experience with the Florida bill "as a catalyst for more meaningful and lasting change." The company would oppose a recent Texas executive order cracking down on parents who provide gender-transitioning medical care for young children and form a companywide task force to develop ways to be a positive force in the LGBT community, Mr. Chapek said.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, Mr. Chapek had apologized for letting his employees down by not taking a public stance before the bill passed and said the company would pause political giving in Florida and do more to advocate against bills across the nation that it views as harmful to the LGBT community. But some employees still feel the company isn't doing enough.\par \par \uc2 "The response internally at the highest levels of the company was not appropriate and completely shortsighted, as well as tone deaf," said Kate Bustamante, senior manager of original documentaries at \uc2 Disney\uc2 's \uc2 Hulu\uc2  streaming service. "I think we need to speak up against those things, because if \uc2 Disney\uc2  wants to continue to be a relevant media company, then they need to get with the program."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Bustamante was among about 50 \uc2 Disney\uc2  employees who gathered Tuesday at Bette Davis Picnic Area in Griffith Park in Glendale, Calif., a short drive from both \uc2 Disney\uc2 's animation studios and its Burbank headquarters.\par \par \uc2 Some carried signs calling on the company to do more to oppose the Florida bill. Several workers who walked off the job said while they have received support from co-workers at the division and team level, they felt corporate leadership has failed them.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Disney\uc2  spokesman said Tuesday that the company had sought to address some of these concerns in Monday's internal meeting. "We know how important this issue is for our LGBTQ+ employees, their families and allies, we respect our colleagues' right to express their views," the spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Several protesters called on \uc2 Disney\uc2  to indefinitely stop, not temporarily pause, political contributions to elected officials involved in the Florida legislation.\par \par \uc2 They also called on the company not to require employees to move. Mr. Chapek was criticized last year for ordering thousands of employees, including the elite group of theme-park engineers known as Imagineers, to relocate from Southern California to Florida to save the company money.\par \par \uc2 "People shouldn't be forced to live in a place that they're fearful of," said Shannon Cheung, who does shipping and logistics for TV shows at ABC Signature, a \uc2 Disney\uc2 -owned studio.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Patience Haggin and Anne Steele contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220323ei3n0001w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
