\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: State Telecommuting Policy Bruises Hartford\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jimmy Vielkind \par \uc2 842 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 March 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As it tries to bounce back from the economic damage caused by the Covid-19 pandemic, Connecticut's capital city of Hartford won't be getting help from one of its biggest employers: the state government itself.\par \par \uc2 Connecticut recently agreed to permanently allow most of its employees to telecommute as many as four days a week, in contrast with many state and local governments that are trying to set an example for private employers by pushing employees to return to offices in downtown cores.\par \par \uc2 As a result, a fraction of the roughly 13,000 state employees who worked in Hartford are returning on any given day and the state sold or ended the lease on four properties in 2020.\par \par \uc2 "It's going to reorder activity," Fred Carstensen, a professor at the \uc2 University of Connecticut\uc2  business school, said of the state's decision to maintain remote work.\par \par \uc2 The loss of foot traffic from Covid-19 lockdowns has already taken its toll on the city, which has around 120,000 residents and, pre-pandemic, a further 100,000 commuters. Twenty-seven percent of city residents said they lost their job since the onset of the pandemic and 31% said they used a food bank, according to a survey released last month by nonprofit research group DataHaven.\par \par \uc2 Occupied downtown office space decreased by 142,000 square feet in 2021 from the prior year, and the average lease length declined from pre-pandemic levels as tenants deferred long-term occupancy plans, according to a recent report by real-estate firm \uc2 CBRE\uc2 . Downtown shops and restaurants have shed employees, reduced hours and in some instances closed to focus on suburban locations.\par \par \uc2 With skyscrapers sprouting a few blocks from the Connecticut River, downtown Hartford has long been a center for commuters even as its population declined 30% from its peak in 1950. The city flirted with bankruptcy in 2017, but was bailed out by the state.\par \par \uc2 In addition to being a center of state government, Hartford is the traditional home to some of the nation's leading insurance companies. Two of them -- Travelers Cos. and \uc2 CVS Health Corp\uc2 ., the parent of \uc2 Aetna\uc2  -- began bringing workers back to the office this week, but not everyone is working all five days, spokespeople said. About 97,000 workers came to the city each day in 2019, of which 71,000 were employed in high-wage jobs, according to U.S. Census data analyzed by DataHaven.\par \par \uc2 Office workers account for the bulk of customers at Professional Barbershop on downtown Pratt Street, said owner Chris Rosa. He said sales haven't gone over half their pre-pandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 "There's just not enough meat to go around the table," he said. "Hartford is a disaster right now." He is thinking of opening another location in a nearby suburb.\par \par \uc2 The state of Connecticut owns about 2.5 million square feet of office space in Hartford, government records show. It leases around 275,000 additional square feet.\par \par \uc2 Connecticut started a pilot program for remote work as part of a 2017 labor contract, but telecommuting was broadly used during the pandemic and remains popular with state workers. Union leaders said their members liked the flexibility, and the administration of Democratic Gov. \uc2 Ned Lamont\uc2  saw savings on real-estate costs.\par \par \uc2 About two-thirds of the state workforce have jobs that can be done remotely and are working from home an average of four days a week, said Josh Geballe, the state's former chief operating officer.\par \par \uc2 "The longer-term model is a small number of larger buildings where we can efficiently share the space among our agencies," Mr. Geballe said.\par \par \uc2 The new system will help the state to attract new employees as it grapples with a potential wave of retirements because of a pension change that takes effect in July, he added.\par \par \uc2 Keith Hill, a supervisor in the Department of Energy and Environmental Protection, said he is now generally in his office in Hartford two days a week instead of the previous five.\par \par \uc2 He said the city isn't as busy as it used to be and food trucks that abounded in the park by his office weren't around last summer.\par \par \uc2 Leaders in other state and local governments, such as Pennsylvania and New York City, have restricted remote work and sent most public employees back to their offices.\par \par \uc2 In response to corporate consolidation and a declining population, Hartford in recent years has focused on developing residential housing to enliven a downtown that has long focused on serving commuters.\par \par \uc2 "We would love to see all employers bring every employee back five days a week, but I think we all recognize that there's been a sea change in the way a lot of employers think about the nature of work," Hartford Mayor \uc2 Luke Bronin\uc2 , a Democrat, said.\par \par \uc2 James Varano said his Hartford restaurant Black-Eyed Sally's is now closed on Mondays and Tuesdays and that downtown residents are a different kind of customer than commuters.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220318ei3i0001r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
