\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Tech Jobs Emerge In Smaller Cities, College Towns\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katherine Bindley \par \uc2 434 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 March 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Where to find new jobs in tech? Increasingly, the answer is far outside Silicon Valley.\par \par \uc2 Smaller cities such as Virginia Beach, Va.; Madison, Wis.; and Durham, N.C., have marked strong growth in new tech jobs while cities traditionally considered tech strongholds haveseen their growth rates slip, according to a \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2  report.\par \par \uc2 Plenty of tech jobs are being created in hubs like San Francisco and San Jose, but the sector's growth has slowed since 2019, the Brookings data show. Gaining in job creation are several Sunbelt cities, college towns and vacation destinations, such as Ogden, Utah, where several hundred new tech jobs boosted the growth rate from flat to 7%. Philadelphia and Minneapolis, and warmer-weather locales like Charlotte, N.C., Nashville, Tenn., and San Antonio, none of them known as traditional tech hiring markets, also notched modest gains in new tech roles.\par \par \uc2 The smaller the city that is reporting tech job growth, the more likely it has to do with remote work, especially these lifestyle places or college towns, says \uc2 Mark Muro\uc2 , a senior researcher with Brookings who co-wrote the report.\par \par \uc2 "To the extent that remote work remains a prevalent way of working in tech, more places may have a shot at building really vibrant local ecosystems," he says.\par \par \uc2 The number of Virginia Beach's new tech jobs rose by 9% in 2020, after falling 4% in 2019. College towns Durham, N.C., and Madison, Wis., experienced tech job growth of 5% and 6%, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Tech jobs in nine cities considered rising stars in technology, including Atlanta, Dallas, Denver, Miami and St. Louis, initially contracted in the early days of the Covid-19 era, but by the end of 2020, tech employment in those cities rose by at least 3% on average. Since then, the number of tech roles posted in these cities has surged, suggesting that workers are attracted to areas with a lower cost of living compared with the East and West Coasts and that companies are tapping the labor market in cities with more diverse workers, Mr. Muro says.\par \par \uc2 For decades, the most valuable tech companies invested heavily in both real estate and talent in the Bay Area and a handful of other cities.\par \par \uc2 These hubs slowed tech hiring with the onset of the pandemic. Austin's job growth rate fell to 3.5% in 2020 from 5.5% in 2019, adding around 3,000 jobs. Los Angeles was nearly flat after having grown by 6% a year for the five years prior.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220310ei3a0001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
