\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Minneapolis Teachers Strike Over Pay, Hiring\b0\par\par\uc2 By Allison Prang \par \uc2 462 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 March 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 More than 4,000 teachers and education support professionals in Minneapolis went on strike Tuesday, pressing school administrators over wages, hiring, class sizes and more resources for mental-health support.\par \par \uc2 Classes were canceled and are expected to remain closed through the duration of the strike, according to Minneapolis Public Schools. The district, with about 28,500 students, has said it is limited financially in how it can address teachers' demands.\par \par \uc2 The Minneapolis Federation of Teachers is calling for the school district to pay competitive salaries for licensed staff members to stop employees from leaving the district, and for education support professionals' starting salaries to be raised to $35,000 a year, from about $24,000. It is also asking the district for better retention and recruitment of educators of color.\par \par \uc2 The union says the district has lost more than 640 educators over the past 18 months.\par \par \uc2 Greta Callahan, president of the union's teachers chapter, said the district has been focused on "power hoarding" and making people do more with less at the bottom.\par \par \uc2 "We're here to say this isn't working," she said in an interview. "You have been hemorrhaging families, you've been hemorrhaging educators. Our students don't have stability. They deserve a high-quality education."\par \par \uc2 Minneapolis Public Schools said the union's decision to strike was disappointing.\par \par \uc2 "We know our organizations' mutual priorities are based on our deep commitment to the education of Minneapolis students," the district said. "MPS will remain at the mediation table nonstop in an effort to reduce the length and impact of this strike."\par \par \uc2 The school district has said it faces a budget gap in revenue and expenses caused by higher costs, a drop in students and long-term underfunding.\par \par \uc2 Schools around the country have been grappling with a growing teacher shortage in recent years. The Covid-19 pandemic stretched many educators thin as districts pivoted to remote work and teachers were forced to learn how to manage classes in online settings, before pivoting back to teaching in person with pandemic protocols.\par \par \uc2 The Minneapolis district has said that for the 2022-23 fiscal year, it is facing a $97.2 million budget shortfall. While it notes it can lower that shortfall substantially with Covid-19 relief funds, it says that money will run out in 2024 and therefore using that money for salaries would worsen its deficit over the long term.\par \par \uc2 Nearby St. Paul Public Schools reached a deal Monday with the St. Paul Federation of Educators, avoiding a strike. The agreement includes keeping caps on class sizes and more mental health support, as well as one-time payments to educators for their work during the pandemic, according to the union.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220309ei3900012\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
