\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Commuter Rail Girds For Long-Term Falloff --- Transit officials are adjusting schedules, unveiling discounts for a decline in ridership\b0\par\par\uc2 By Scott Calvert and Jimmy Vielkind \par \uc2 673 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The nation's biggest commuter railroads are preparing for potentially permanent shifts in daily ridership, declines that in some cases could threaten their long-term viability.\par \par \uc2 The changes are based on expectations that many office workers will continue to work from home at least part-time for years after the pandemic subsides. Transit officials in some cities have shifted schedules away from traditional rush-hour periods, or are doing more to attract suburbanites headed downtown for sporting events as a way to fill seats.\par \par \uc2 Agencies are also rolling out discounted ticket packages geared to occasional commuters, who no longer need to shell out hundreds of dollars for monthly passes that formed the bedrock of the commuter-railroad business model.\par \par \uc2 Ridership on some U.S. commuter railroads has ticked up as the Omicron variant faded, but largely empty trains are common, according to data from the \uc2 American Public Transportation Association\uc2 . The most recently reported weekday passenger counts at the nation's five largest systems ranged between 25% and 55% of pre-pandemic levels, the systems said.\par \par \uc2 The falloff for commuter rail has been far steeper and more sustained than for other transit modes like municipal bus service, in part because many front-line workers who don't have a remote option rely on the bus or subway to get to their jobs.\par \par \uc2 "We already know that the traditional commuting patterns have probably gone out the window. But that doesn't mean that people can't utilize transit for social activity," said Debra A. Johnson, chief executive of Denver's Regional Transportation District.\par \par \uc2 Billions of dollars in federal Covid-19 aid have helped sustain transit agencies, but that money will run out in coming years. The Massachusetts Bay Transportation Authority, which operates commuter rail and other transit modes around Boston, predicts it will exhaust the nearly $2 billion it received sometime in the 2023-24 fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 The MBTA commuter rail system is averaging about 45,000 weekday passenger trips versus roughly 120,000 before Covid-19, and officials don't expect a full rebound.\par \par \uc2 "We're going to have to figure out a way to operate with lower fare revenue, and it remains to be seen how much lower it's going to be," MBTA General Manager \uc2 Steve Poftak\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Caltrain, whose commuter trains link San Francisco with Silicon Valley, said in January that a sustained ridership decline "poses a severe financial challenge to the viability of the railroad's business model." Caltrain said fare revenue historically covered nearly 70% of operating costs.\par \par \uc2 The report cited a survey in which 37% of people said they expected to commute at least four days a week post-pandemic, down from 71% before, while 41% said they would likely commute a few days weekly or monthly, up from 9% pre-pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Sebastian Petty, Caltrain's deputy chief of planning, said the agency, like the MBTA, was pivoting to focus less on the commuter market. "Our future is to be more of a regional rail provider," he said.\par \par \uc2 Metra's Chicago-area commuter service is carrying about 25% of its usual passenger load. The agency projects it will take until the end of 2024 to hit 80% of its previous ridership, CEO Jim Derwinski said.\par \par \uc2 Sales of monthly passes at the three commuter railroads serving New York City -- the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2 , Metro-North Railroad and \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2  -- fell more than 95% early in the pandemic. Since then, sales haven't exceeded 30% of pre-pandemic levels, according to an analysis by The Wall Street Journal. Ridership is around 50% of pre-pandemic levels, says the \uc2 Metropolitan Transportation Authority\uc2 , which oversees the LIRR and Metro-North.\par \par \uc2 This month the \uc2 MTA\uc2  unveiled a 20-trip ticket that will give riders a 20% per-ride discount from the single-ticket peak fare, a move aimed at former monthly pass holders.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220307ei370001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
