\par\par \b \uc2 REVIEW --- Moving Targets: Some Jobs Are Just Made to Resign From\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Queenan \par \uc2 711 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 March 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par C6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Millions of Americans have walked away from their jobs in a startling phenomenon known as the "Great Resignation." They're sick of commuting, sick of dreary working conditions, sick of their bosses. Whether nurses, waiters, truck drivers or knowledge workers, they've had it. They're going home, and they're not coming back.\par \par \uc2 The Great Resignation has now begun to spread to some unusual sectors of the economy.\par \par \uc2 Members of tribute bands, for instance, are folding their tents for good. "I couldn't play 'Hotel California' even one more time," says Cheyenne Foucault-Giordano, lead guitarist in No, Not the \uc2 Philadelphia Eagles\uc2 , an Omaha group that's been playing exclusively the music of the Eagles since 1983. "Now, with what I can pick up as an \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver, I'll never have to play 'Life in the Fast Lane' again. Talk about a peaceful, easy feeling -- this is it."\par \par \uc2 Economists have identified another unexpected field where the Great Resignation is reshaping the labor force. "Nightclub bouncers are throwing in the towel in droves," says an econometrician from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . "It's scary work, it pays nothing, there's no 401(k) package, and there's always some cheap punk who wants to pick a fight with you. It's the same thing with the security guards at mixed martial-arts events. The hours are brutal, you never get to meet any nice people, and there's no future in it."\par \par \uc2 The same thing has happened with stand-up comics, mimes and rodeo clowns, not to mention those people who walk around in unlicensed costumes and body paint taking tips to pose for photos with tourists.\par \par \uc2 "Now that I can spend the entire day dressed up as the Statue of Liberty but do it on Zoom, I'll never work Times Square again," says a middle-aged woman who has also logged time as Uncle Sam, Cleopatra and Tickle Me Elmo. "These days, I can stay home and entertain my kids. Not that they enjoy my work."\par \par \uc2 Sideline reporters at football games have also embraced the Great Resignation. Says one veteran member of the profession: "You stand in the freezing rain all afternoon, waiting for a chance to ask the losing coach 'Did you make any adjustments at halftime?' The players despise you, the fans pour beer all over your clothes, and the real announcers up in the booth look down on you. An awful lot of us are switching over to putting up Sheetrock, where at least your clients value your work."\par \par \uc2 A surprisingly large contingent in another job -- punditry -- has also walked away. As one of these sage observers of the human condition puts it: "There's only so many times you can repeat the words 'This should be done sooner rather than later' or 'The center will not hold' before you start hating yourself. The hours are brutal, your kids think you're a pathetic loser, and you have to spend too much time interviewing brain-dead politicians who regurgitate things like 'Those who cannot remember the past are condemned to repeat it.' Trust me on this, at the end of the day, make no mistake, all things considered: I'd rather deliver food for Fresh Direct."\par \par \uc2 Are there other professions where the Great Resignation is rapidly spreading? Oh, yes. Battle-worn pediatricians who admit that they never really liked kids. Dentists suffering from torn rotator cuffs and excruciating carpal tunnel syndrome. Dejected psychologists working with self-indulgent neurotics who are far beyond repair. And even professional dowsers who admit that spending your entire life listening for water-main leaks in empty streets in the middle of the night puts too much strain on a marriage.\par \par \uc2 That said, there is one profession where people are definitely not joining in the Great Resignation: economics.\par \par \uc2 "I get paid good money to be consistently wrong about interest rates, inflation, oil prices and GDP," says a well-remunerated member of the club who last made an accurate prediction in 1997. "And if I get it wrong, I can always blame it on the Fed. So why would I even think about giving up this gig?"\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220305ei350000r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
