\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 On The Clock: Worker Romances Bloomed on Zoom --- Some colleagues got close even while many offices were shut during the pandemic, and more may be eyeing each other as they return\b0\par\par\uc2 By Callum Borchers \par \uc2 993 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 March 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers trickling back to offices may notice a few changes: Hot desks. More meeting spaces. And, wait, Tammy in sales is with Carl from accounting now?\par \par \uc2 While you've been Zooming and Slacking, more colleagues than you might think have been coupling. A third of workers said they were, or have been, involved with a colleague when the \uc2 Society for Human Resource Management\uc2  conducted a survey in January -- up from roughly a quarter in early 2020.\par \par \uc2 And co-worker mating season may be just beginning.\par \par \uc2 "There's a lot of loneliness, and a little bit of desperation, that goes with being a single person during the pandemic," says Michael Rosenfeld, a Stanford sociologist who studies how couples meet. For much of the past two years, he adds, co-workers have been "among the few people you have real interaction with."\par \par \uc2 As many companies plan office returns in the coming weeks, co-workers may be eyeing each other with renewed interest, whether because of genuine connections formed on conference calls or the novelty of seeing someone -- anyone -- in person again.\par \par \uc2 Office romance was on the wane when the pandemic struck, rendered obsolete by the popularity of digital matchmaking and cautionary tales from the #MeToo movement. Going remote would finish the job, we assumed.\par \par \uc2 Instead, two years of relative isolation, the very thing that could have ended dating among co-workers, appears to be reviving it.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rosenfeld's research shows roughly 1 in 5 couples met through work in the mid-1990s, but that rate had been halved by 2017, as Tinder, eHarmony and similar services became the most common way to get together.\par \par \uc2 #MeToo underscored the potential pitfalls of pursuing a colleague, and some companies responded by restricting relationships or mandating disclosures. \uc2 Jeff Zucker\uc2 's recent exit as president of CNN is yet another reminder that mixing the professional with the personal can have severe consequences.\par \par \uc2 Yet in the work-from-home age, a screenshot brought Gregory Kelley and Marwah Rizqy together.\par \par \uc2 "I think I'm in love with Marwah," Mr. Kelley confided in a text to a colleague, who sensed the crush was mutual and forwarded a picture of the message to Ms. Rizqy.\par \par \uc2 A Covid-era courtship ensued -- video chats progressing to outdoor walks and eventually indoor dates -- and the two recently married.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kelley and Ms. Rizqy, both 36, were elected to the National Assembly of Quebec at the same time, in 2018, and had formed a friendship but nothing more -- until pandemic-induced separation awakened something stronger.\par \par \uc2 "I started FaceTiming Greg more often and, at that time, I realized I had feelings for him," Ms. Rizqy says.\par \par \uc2 Other office romances kindled before the pandemic grew more serious in a trying time. Matt Zalucki, a 34-year-old software consultant in Albany, N.Y., says he took things slow when a project manager named Kaylee Urban first interested him in 2018. He worried about misreading her signals but finally made a move when he was sure she'd say yes.\par \par \uc2 The couple had recently moved in together when offices closed, in March 2020. Mr. Zalucki says those early months in lockdown convinced him Ms. Urban was the one.\par \par \uc2 "Working from home and being together in the pandemic definitely solidified that I wanted to do this," says Mr. Zalucki, who proposed that summer. "It would have probably shown me the other way, if it was that case."\par \par \uc2 The wedding is planned for November.\par \par \uc2 Frontline\uc2  workers are finding love on the job, too, even if they had to stay 6 feet apart and seldom saw each other's unmasked faces for many months.\par \par \uc2 Hannah Maixner, a 26-year-old grocery clerk in Missouri, recently celebrated a first anniversary with her boyfriend and frequent shift mate, and says quite a few of her colleagues are dating each other.\par \par \uc2 People who kept showing up when much of the world shut down bonded over the experience -- sometimes in surprising ways, Ms. Maixner says. She worked with her boyfriend for six years before their first date and initially considered him "super weird." As her social circle shrank outside of work and she got to know him better, she grew attracted to his kindness.\par \par \uc2 We have mixed feelings about relationships among our colleagues. Three-quarters of workers say they don't have a problem with people dating each other at work, according to the SHRM poll. Yet 77% of those who've had flings with co-workers say they've concealed them.\par \par \uc2 Anyone who's paid a modicum of attention to the #MeToo movement can appreciate some of the qualms. Beyond sexual harassment and quid pro quos, even healthy relationships can fuel perceptions of favoritism. Bad breakups and even friendly ones can complicate business.\par \par \uc2 Many HR departments say relationships among colleagues are not top concerns at the moment, given worker safety issues and staffing shortages.\par \par \uc2 "Does it make the list of things that I'm worried about? No," says Fran Katsoudas, who heads human resources at \uc2 Cisco Systems Inc\uc2 ., where she's an executive vice president.\par \par \uc2 She adds that HR chiefs do consider whether there may be a power imbalance, with one partner wielding authority over the other.\par \par \uc2 Some people who've pledged not to date co-workers are reconsidering their positions. Joanna Tjiu was fully remote until her company recently transferred her to Malaysia, where the 25-year-old lawyer reports to an office twice a week. It didn't take long for a male colleague to strike her fancy.\par \par \uc2 "It was like the first time looking at a new human," Ms. Tjiu says.\par \par \uc2 Though she knows there could be risks, she's "been dropping small hints. Just in case the other person has even a slight interest in me."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220303ei330000a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
