\par\par \b \uc2 C-Suite Strategies --- When Your Employee Wants a Special Workplace Deal: More bosses should be saying yes. One size doesn't fit all.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Denise M. Rousseau and Laurie R. Weingart \par \uc2 1,589 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 February 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Since the pandemic began, more employees want to be exceptions to the rule.\par \par \uc2 They want personalized arrangements that differ from the ones their co-workers have -- from remote work to a tailored schedule to a reworked job that is better aligned with their interests and goals.\par \par \uc2 But many managers have no idea how to make these deals work, or whether to even consider them in the first place. They are used to a one-size-fits-all workplace, which on the surface, at least, seems easier and fairer. And they worry that making a mistake with these personal deals will lead to tension and resentment from other employees.\par \par \uc2 But having spent our careers studying how individual workers negotiate what we call "i-deals," we believe the opposite is true: These deals can make the workplace a fairer environment, and make it more likely that employees do their best work.\par \par \uc2 If, that is, bosses handle these negotiations the right way. Here's how they can make that happen.\par \par \uc2 Why make deals?\par \par \uc2 Let's look at why employers should consider i-deals in the first place.\par \par \uc2 In an era when more people are switching jobs, personalized deals can help bosses hold on to valued employees, by giving them opportunities and arrangements they can't find elsewhere. The best managers realize that one size doesn't fit all and that employees thrive under the conditions best for them. For instance, you might grant an employee special flexibility to work around an elderly parent's care. A consultant working on Korean-language fluency might be allowed to specialize in clients based in Korea.\par \par \uc2 When you don't allow exceptions, many people will end up being less productive and less happy than they could be.\par \par \uc2 In addition, one-time i-deals may inspire new practices and policies that help the whole company. In one case, a technology company was bargaining with a sales representative who wanted reduced travel. In the process of tailoring the job, the business created new ways of demonstrating products remotely -- methods that any sales rep could use. The new systems ended up doing a better job of engaging customers.\par \par \uc2 In another instance, an IT professional asking for advanced education in healthcare systems inspired the company to start a new service line for the whole company -- offering technical tools to support clinical decision making. The deal was granted to reward a hardworking professional, but the new service line was an unanticipated benefit.\par \par \uc2 Don't just think hours and money\par \par \uc2 I-deals are about more than money and work hours. They can be about how jobs are structured or work is done, capitalizing on the uniqueness of individuals. Expatriates bargain to move horses and grand pianos on their overseas assignments. Office workers negotiate to eliminate filing interim reports to bosses, or substitute a monthly check-in. IT professionals bargain to do their jobs from the Bahamas instead of Omaha, to come to the office in sweats instead of suits, or to take off two months a year to go mountaineering.\par \par \uc2 Think subtraction -- not just addition\par \par \uc2 Research indicates that workers granted i-deals for their growth and advancement can experience increased workloads. Yet bosses are often blind to the idea of subtraction -- that is, removing noncritical tasks from a person's responsibilities. Addition tends to be preferred especially by managers, because adding something often feels easier and less disruptive than taking something away. It's as if they feel that special deals shouldn't be accompanied by fewer things to do.\par \par \uc2 But subtraction can help support the new deal, and will be especially appreciated by valuable employees. As a result, bosses should be open if an employee wants to drop tasks that aren't mission critical, that someone else can do more easily or that interfere with career development.\par \par \uc2 Be prepared to experiment\par \par \uc2 Because i-deals are exceptions, their benefits and costs cannot be completely anticipated, so framing the negotiation as an experiment or pilot makes the process easier. It lets the company and employee revisit deals over time to see how well they are working -- and redesigning or sunsetting them accordingly. Having this process in place also gives co-workers a greater sense of fairness, since a process exists to address potential problems. In the case of a manager who negotiated to go part time to spend more time at home with his young son, his boss asked for a review of the arrangement every three months. Over time, the part-time manager adjusted his schedule and ways of working to help things run more smoothly -- including better developing staff to make more decisions themselves.\par \par \uc2 Be transparent\par \par \uc2 A department head in our university once told us that if he wouldn't be comfortable posting a deal on the office discussion board, he probably shouldn't make it. His business manager records the terms of any i-deal, so if co-workers get wind of a deal and want one too, the features of the deal need to hold up to scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 In addition, though you can't always share the specifics of an employee's i-deal due to privacy concerns -- particularly where health issues are involved -- you should be clear about the circumstances that warrant an i-deal. Too many managers follow the policy of "don't ask, don't tell," but secrecy undermines trust and fairness. Get the basic requirements for granting an i-deal out front in conversations with employees and at group meetings. And if several people want something that cannot be given to all, consider whether turn-taking might be appropriate, like rotating opportunities to attend conferences or high-profile meetings.\par \par \uc2 In general, we find that physical and mental-health reasons for i-deals are broadly acceptable to other employees, particularly if the individual was in good standing before the health issues arose. Likewise, i-deals in the form of special work arrangements granted following completion of a challenging project or busy year signal respect for high contribution just as does offering special flexibility to ease a person's acceptance of new responsibilities.\par \par \uc2 Be honest when you say 'no'\par \par \uc2 Transparency is also crucial when you give one employee an i-deal but must turn down another's request. First, ask yourself: Is the person's performance or reliability the issue? Or do work requirements mean you can only grant flexibility to a few?\par \par \uc2 In either case, your explanation should be clear and justifiable. If you would grant flexibility were performance or reliability not an issue, tell the person so. Instead of "No," consider saying "Not yet" and lay out the work behavior the person must demonstrate to justify a special deal, then follow-up.\par \par \uc2 Let's say two people want to work from home but you only trust one to work well remotely. Make it clear that this special arrangement is granted to high performers who work well on their own. The less-reliable worker might be offered the possibility of working from home in the future if they improve.\par \par \uc2 But if real constraints exist on how many special arrangements you can grant, make clear to all employees the conditions that warrant making an exception to the rule. You need a clear policy based on principles, and stick to them. Special arrangements for workers in good standing, with seniority or exceptional needs tend to be viewed as legitimate.\par \par \uc2 When workers perceive the process as fair, they are more likely to view your decision as fair even if it isn't what they wanted to hear. No one needs to feel embarrassed for obtaining an i-deal, or jealous or angry of others who do, when they know that they were obtained via an open process available to all.\par \par \uc2 Be mindful of the organization's values\par \par \uc2 Granting a deal that goes against organizational values or norms of conduct is unlikely to be acceptable to peers. One company we know of moved an expatriate manager's family overseas along with his mistress and her horse. When the deal became public, it became a symbol of managerial excess and favoritism, and was deeply resented.\par \par \uc2 What's more, be careful about rewarding people who use i-deals as a bargaining tactic. Peers often resent someone who gets more money after threatening to quit, while their salaries stay the same. The fact the person with the raise had a job offer elsewhere can make the situation worse, since it punishes people who cannot easily pick up and move. Is the person with the job offer really more productive than peers?\par \par \uc2 Rewarding people for mobility alone also plays down loyalty and the contributions that others continue to make without a raise in pay -- making the whole situation more inequitable. And, of course, rewarding employees who repeatedly try to get special deals is a surefire way to create even more co-worker resentment. Such repeat bargainers typically ask for the things most likely to annoy their peers.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Dr. Rousseau is the H.J. Heinz II university professor of organizational behavior and public policy at \uc2 Carnegie Mellon University\uc2 's Heinz College of Information Systems and Public Policy and the Tepper School of Business. Dr. Weingart is the Richard M. and Margaret S. Cyert professor of organizational behavior and theory at the Tepper School of Business. They can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220222ei2m0000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
